    "file": "../artifacts/12-platform-policy-memo.md",
    "content": "# 12: Platform Policy Memo\n\n## Metadata\n\n- Source type: internal policy memo\n- Organization: Threadline Trust Operations\n- Subject: High-Value Departure Events\n- Date: 2 February 2027\n- Classification: internal\n- Status: excerpt\n\n## Artifact\n\n### High-Value Departure Events: Continuity and Harm Reduction\n\nCreator departure events can produce community instability, copycat withdrawal, monetization disruption, impersonation attempts, and narrative capture by hostile actors.\n\nThis is especially likely when the departing creator has:\n\n- High trust density\n- Strong parasocial identification\n- Ideological framing around exit, refusal, or anti-platform life\n- Paid community infrastructure\n- Archived voice, writing, and interaction data sufficient for continuity tooling\n\nRecommended response:\n\n1. Preserve account integrity where possible.\n2. Reduce virality of location-seeking and exit-instruction content.\n3. Maintain community channels under moderator or platform stewardship.\n4. Offer archive transition options.\n5. Avoid language that frames departure as liberation.\n6. Where appropriate, support creator-presence continuity through approved representation tools.\n\n### Risk Language\n\nDo not use:\n\n- disappearance\n- escape\n- liberation\n- offline refuge\n- proof of exit\n\nUse:\n\n- sabbatical\n- privacy reset\n- creator recovery\n- continuity\n- sustainable participation\n- community care\n\n### Notes\n\nThe goal is not to compel return. The goal is to prevent an individual exit from becoming a symbolic exit pathway for others.\n\n## Investigator Notes\n\nThe memo does not mention Mara by name, but the timing and criteria align closely with her departure.\n\nThe language recommended here appears in the 2030 AI summary and returned account messaging.\n\n\"Approved representation tools\" is the first institutional bridge between departure management and synthetic continuity.\n\n"
